{"name":"Glamour Green","hex":"#007488","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"glamour","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Originally, enchantment; magic charm; especially, the effect of a spell that causes one to see objects in a form that differs from reality, typically to make filthy, ugly, or repulsive things seem beauteous.","Alluring beauty or charm (often with sex appeal)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To enchant; to bewitch.","↑ Postrel, Virginia (5 November 2013), “One: The Magic of Glamour”, in The Power of Glamour: Longing and the Art of Visual Persuasion, Simon & Schuster, →ISBN: “Reflecting this sense of the word, by 1902 Webster's included two new definitions: “a kind of haze in the air, causing things to appear different from what they really are”…”"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/glamour"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Glamour","blocks":[{"text":"In the field of cultural studies, glamour is the impression of attraction or fascination that a particularly luxurious or elegant appearance creates, an impression which intensifies reality. Usually, a person, event, location, technology, or product such as a piece of clothing can be glamorous or add glamour. \"Glamour\" originally referred to a magic spell, an illusion said to be cast by witches."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a4/Lana_Turner_by_Paul_Hesse_1946.jpg/330px-Lana_Turner_by_Paul_Hesse_1946.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glamour","wikidata":"Q390104"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Chrysler_building-_top.jpg/500px-Chrysler_building-_top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Chrysler_building-_top.jpg/500px-Chrysler_building-_top.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Art deco, as seen here on the Chrysler Building  in Midtown Manhattan, is often characterised as glamorous.[2]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChrysler_building-_top.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Aston.db5.coupe.300pix.jpg/250px-Aston.db5.coupe.300pix.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_co